{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The righteous who walks in his integrity — happy are his children after him (Proverbs 20:7).</b>" "<b>Solomon,</b> of blessed memory, informed us in this verse (Proverbs 20:7) that a person is not righteous until he walks in his integrity in the service of the blessed Name. And the meaning of 'walks in his integrity' is that a person should perform the commandments as required and intend in them love and fear of Him — not to aggrandize himself, not to boast, and not so that through this he will be more honored in the eyes of people. For a person ought to do good deeds and kindness according to his ability, and not boast of them, and exert himself in good deeds and not recount them on his lips. And anyone who recounts them on his lips and publicizes them and praises himself in them — that person is a sinner, and two evils result from this: first, that he grows prideful in his recounting to the one who received his words and believed him; and second, that he shames and embarrasses the one who received the kindness from him. And the measure of the righteous man who walks in his integrity is that he does not boast of his virtues and the righteous deeds he has done — and behold, he is called 'a man of faithfulness' (איש אמונים). Therefore Solomon, of blessed memory, joined this verse and bound it to the verse above it, which says (Proverbs 20:6): 'רב אדם יקרא איש חסדו ואיש אמונים מי ימצא' — Most people proclaim each one's kindness, but a man of faithfulness — who can find? That is to say, it is the way of most people to proclaim and publicize all the kindness and good that they do, but a man of faithfulness who does kindnesses and good deeds and does not publicize them but covers and conceals them — who can find? And he mentioned איש אמונים in the language of (Proverbs 11:13): 'ונאמן רוח מכסה דבר' — and a faithful spirit conceals a matter. And this is what Solomon, of blessed memory, said in another place concerning this very matter (Proverbs 10:8): 'חכם לב יקח מצות ואויל שפתים ילבט' — A wise heart takes commandments, but a fool of lips is ruined. He said that one who is wise of heart takes the commandments in his hands and brings them to action and does not recount them on his lips, and the fool is his complete opposite: not enough that he does not exert himself in the performance of commandments to take them in his hands, but at the time he exerts himself in the performance of transgressions — for which he is called a fool — he makes them known on his lips and publicizes them to all. And behold, both are the opposite of one another: this one takes the commandments in his hand and conceals them, and that one exerts himself in evil deeds and reveals them. And from this it says ילבט, that is to say, he is caught by his lips — and it is from the language of (Hosea 4:14): 'ועם לא יבין ילבט,' and Jonathan translated it: וסכלא בשפוותיה מתאחד — and the fool is entangled in his lips. 'Happy are his children after him' — for he is what brings merit to his seed after him, in the manner that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'ועושה חסד לאלפים לעושים מאהבה' — and showing kindness to the thousandth generation for those who act out of love; 'ולשומרי מצותיו לאלף דור לעושים מיראה' — and to those who keep His commandments to the thousandth generation for those who act out of fear. This one depends on this, and that one depends on that. And thus Solomon, of blessed memory, explained in another place that the merit of the righteous benefits his seed after him, as he said (Proverbs 14:26): 'ביראת ה׳ מבטח עוז ולבניו יהיה מחסה' — In the fear of YHWH is a stronghold of confidence, and for His children there is a refuge. And it is possible to interpret that this verse comes to praise three virtues, each one above the other. He mentioned first מתהלך, and the virtue of 'walking' is a great virtue among the righteous, and behold it is above the rank of integrity (תמימות), and the rank of integrity is above the rank of the righteous (צדיק), for there is a righteous man who is not תמים, who has not reached the rank of integrity. And these are the order of the virtues: after one has reached the measure of צדיק, he ascends to the measure of תמים, and from the measure of תמים he ascends to the measure of 'walking' (התהלכות). And all three of these are found in Noah, as it is written." "<b>These are the generations of Noah: Noah was a righteous man, wholehearted in his generations; Noah walked with Elohim.</b> תולדות — in the sense of occurrence, as it is written (Proverbs 27:1): 'כי לא תדע מה ילד יום' — for you do not know what a day will bring forth — since afterward he mentions 'ויולד נח' — and Noah begot — which are the generations. And Scripture mentioned concerning this righteous man three virtues of praise that were the opposite of the men of his generation. The first: that he was righteous (צדיק) in his deeds, not a man of violence (איש חמס) like the men of his generation, of whom it is written: 'ותמלא הארץ חמס' — and the earth was filled with violence. The second: that he was wholehearted (תמים), that is, perfect in all his character traits. For an animal perfect without blemish, Scripture calls תמימה, in the language of the verse (Numbers 19:2): 'פרה אדומה תמימה אשר אין בה מום' — a red heifer, wholehearted, in which there is no blemish. And the blemish (מום) of a person is a disreputable character trait. Therefore Scripture mentioned of Noah that he was תמים — wholehearted, perfect, with no blemish, for there was in him no disreputable trait. And Scripture says (Psalms 119:1): 'אשרי תמימי דרך' — Happy are those whose way is wholehearted — and its meaning is: wholehearted in character traits. And this is the opposite of the men of his generation, who were possessors of disreputable traits, as it is written of them: 'כי השחית כל בשר' and so forth — for all flesh had corrupted its way. The third: 'את האלהים התהלך נח' — Noah walked with Elohim. And it is known that the trait of 'walking' (התהלכות) we have found only in the unique individuals of the generation. And what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 13:5): 'אחרי ה׳ אלהיכם תלכו' — after HaShem your Elohim you shall walk — and it did not say 'תתהלכו,' is because it speaks to all Israel collectively, and this great virtue exists only among the select individuals, the treasured ones of each and every generation. Furthermore, this expression applies only to the righteous man who attained knowledge of the course of the sun (השמש) and the powers of the spheres and the stars, and how they produce effects in the lower realm. For with this knowledge, the greatness and exaltedness of the Master of all is made known — as David said (Psalms 19:2): 'השמים מספרים כבוד אל ומעשה ידיו מגיד הרקיע' — The heavens recount the glory of El, and the firmament declares the work of His hands. And from this the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Anyone who knows how to calculate the seasons (תקופות) and constellations (מזלות) and does not calculate them — concerning him Scripture says (Isaiah 5:12): ואת פועל ה׳ לא יביטו' and so forth — and the work of His hands they do not regard. And it is written (Psalms 82:5): 'לא ידעו ולא יבינו בחשכה יתהלכו' — They do not know and do not understand; in darkness they walk. Therefore it says of Noah: 'את האלהים התהלך נח' — Noah walked with Elohim — for he attained knowledge of the course of the sun and its mighty powers, and he knew the powers of the stars and constellations, and did not turn his heart after them, for he knew in his wisdom that there is a supernal cause above them all, and he cleaved to it. And this is the opposite of the men of his generation, who were worshippers of the sun and were sunk in the worship of stars and constellations, making them deities. And this is what is written of them: 'ותשחת הארץ' — and the earth was corrupted — for the inclination of the mind after idolatry is called corruption (השחתה), in the manner of what is written (Exodus 32:7): 'כי שחת עמך' — for your people has corrupted itself. And from this Scripture called Jeroboam, who worshipped idolatry, 'איש משחית' — a corrupting man. And it clarified that the corruption was 'לפני האלהים' — before Elohim — in the manner of what is written concerning idolatry (Exodus 20:3): 'לא יהיה לך אלהים אחרים על פני' — You shall have no other elohim before My face. And this virtue we found first in Enoch, and he is the first and foremost in it, of whom it is written: 'ויתהלך חנוך את האלהים' — and Enoch walked with Elohim — for he observed the generation of Enosh, who were worshippers of the sun and the stars and the constellations, and he grasped that wisdom fully, and he knew and attained that there is a First Supernal Cause above everything. And through his two life-spans, which were 365 years, equal to the number of the days of the solar year, Scripture hinted to us that he attained in his wisdom knowledge of the course of the sun and cleaved to the Supernal Cause, blessed be He. Also concerning Abraham, Scripture mentioned regarding him: 'התהלך לפני והיה תמים' — Walk before Me and be wholehearted — from what is known that he debated with many nations concerning matters of faith and its laws and statutes, that he would constantly contend with the people of Ur Kasdim, his birthplace, who were all worshippers of the sun, and they would say of him that he was a god, and they would bring great proofs for their words, and when they saw with their own eyes his great and mighty power in the lower realm — and Abraham would acknowledge this to them, that is, the greatness of His power — but like an axe in the hand of the craftsman. Therefore he surrendered his soul to the fiery furnace, and this is: 'אשר הוצאתיך מאור כשדים' — who brought you out of the fire of the Chaldeans." "<b>In his generations.</b> It should have said בדורו — in his generation. But when the Flood came he was six hundred years old, and many generations had already passed over him, and Scripture tells you that all those generations corrupted themselves, and in none of them was there anyone worthy of being saved except him." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason Scripture mentioned Noah three times corresponds to the three virtues it mentioned — צדיק, תמים, and התהלך נח — to indicate that he was perfect in all three of them." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> because He saw three worlds — He saw the world inhabited, and destroyed, and inhabited."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:11:19.277392272Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}